# Recipe: a shipping label, barcodes, ZPL

A 4 × 6 in label for a thermal printer, in black and white, with a Code 128 and a QR code, as a PDF
and as ZPL. The example in the gem: `stationery examples shipping_label --source`; the packing slip
(`stationery examples packing_slip --source`) draws a barcode on a landscape page.

```ruby
class ShippingLabelPdf < Stationery::Document
  page size: :label_4x6, margin: mm(4)                # or "4in x 6in", or [mm(100), mm(150)]
  monochrome dpi: 203                                 # warn about anything a 203 dpi printer cannot print
  print scaling: :none                                # no "fit to page" in the print dialog
  default_text size: 10
  metadata title: "Shipping label"

  def self.preview
    new(tracking: "SX0042771903", service: "EXPRESS",
        to: ["Ada Example", "12 Sample Street", "3011 Testville", "Netherlands"])
  end

  def initialize(tracking:, service:, to:)
    super()
    @tracking = tracking
    @service = service
    @to = to
  end

  def view_template
    box(background: "#000000", padding: [8, 10]) do
      text @service, size: 18, weight: :bold, color: "#FFFFFF", align: :center
    end
    spacer 10
    text "SHIP TO", size: 8, weight: :bold
    text @to.join("\n"), size: 14, leading: 2
    spacer 12
    rule height: 2
    spacer 12
    row(gap: 12, align: :middle) do
      column { text "Scan to track", size: 9 }
      column(width: :auto) { barcode "https://track.example/#{@tracking}", type: :qr, module_size: 2, native: true }
    end
    spacer 12
    box(border: { width: 2 }, padding: 10) do
      barcode @tracking, type: :code128, height: 60, align: :center, native: true
      spacer 4
      text @tracking, size: 12, weight: :bold, letter_spacing: 1, align: :center
    end
  end
end

label = ShippingLabelPdf.preview
label.to_pdf("label.pdf")                             # to look at, or to print from a browser
label.to_zpl("label.zpl")                             # ZPL II for the printer, one ^XA…^XZ per page
```

## Verify a label

A label that does not fit its page does not warn: what does not fit moves to a second page, `strict`
does not catch it, and `to_zpl` writes **two labels**. This recipe leaves about 64 pt (22 mm, three
lines of 14 pt text) free under the Code 128 box; a return address or a longer address uses it up.
After every change:

1. **One page.** `label.to_zpl.scan("^XA").size == 1`, or `have_page_count(1)` in a spec; from the
   shell, `stationery render label_pdf.rb --png` writes `label_pdf-1.png` and a `label_pdf-2.png`
   when it spilled. Make room by shrinking type, spacers or the barcode's `height:`, not by hoping.
2. **Look at the printer's picture**: `label.to_png("label.png", monochrome: true)` (one bit, at
   the class's `monochrome dpi:`), and open it.
3. **Decode the barcodes** from that picture: `zbarimg -q label.png` (`brew install zbar`) prints
   `CODE-128:SX0042771903` and `QR-Code:https://…`. A code it cannot read will not scan either.
4. **Check the native commands** are in the ZPL: `grep -o '\^B[CQEX][^^]*' label.zpl` shows `^BC`
   (Code 128), `^BQ` (QR), `^BE` (EAN-13), `^BX` (Data Matrix). A barcode that is missing there
   stayed in the picture (see below), which prints too, but not on the printer's own dot grid.
5. **Warnings**: `label.warnings` after `to_zpl` (or `to_png`) lists what a one-bit printer
   cannot print as it is.

There is no ZPL renderer in the gem and none is needed offline: the `^GFA` graphic of each label
is, dot for dot, the `to_png(monochrome: true)` picture of the same document at the same dpi,
except where native barcodes go, which the graphic leaves white and the printer fills with its own
encoder. So the PNG shows the native barcodes as stationery draws them: the same data and place,
possibly another QR mask. (Online, labelary.com renders a `.zpl` file.)

## What matters here

- **The page is the label**: `page size: :label_4x6` (also `:label_4x3`, `:label_4x2`,
  `:label_100x150`, `:label_100x50`, or any `"102mm x 74mm"`) with a small margin. Lengths are
  points; `mm()` and `inch()` convert.
- **`barcode(data, type:)`**: `:code128` (the default; printable ASCII), `:ean13` (12 digits and
  the check digit), `:qr` (`level: :l, :m, :q, :h`) or `:datamatrix`. It is vector bars with its
  quiet zone included. Size a QR code by `module_size:` (points a module) or `width:`, and a linear
  code by `module_size:` or `width:` and `height:`; it shrinks to the space it is given. No font or
  gem is needed.
- **ZPL**: `to_zpl` draws each page one bit to a dot and sends it as a graphic, so any font and
  layout prints as the PDF shows it. `dpi:` is the printer's (152, 203, 300, 600; 203 by default,
  or the class's `monochrome dpi:`), `copies:`, `pages:`. `native: true` on a barcode (or
  `to_zpl(native: true)`) has the printer draw it with its own `^BC` / `^BQ` command, crisp on its
  dot grid. A barcode the printer could not draw as it is stays in the picture without a word: one
  rotated or clipped, paler than half grey, a module wider than 10 dots (a QR code sized with a
  large `width:` at 300 dpi), or a QR code of text outside ASCII. Size a native QR code by
  `module_size:` (1 to 2 pt) and check the ZPL has its `^BQ` line.
  Send it to port 9100: `TCPSocket.open(host, 9100) { |s| label.to_zpl(s) }`.
  From the shell: `stationery render label_pdf.rb --zpl --dpi 300`.
- **Black and white only.** A thermal printer prints black or nothing. `monochrome dpi: 203` reports
  every grey, colour, opacity and line thinner than a dot as a warning; `monochrome snap: true`
  changes them instead (dark to black, light left out). White text on a black box is fine.
- **Look at what the printer will print**: `label.to_png("label.png", monochrome: true)` draws the
  one-bit picture at the printer's dpi; open it and check the bars are clean and nothing is cut off.
  `stationery render label_pdf.rb --png` draws the PDF.
- **Printing the PDF** from a desktop: `print scaling: :none` keeps the dialog from shrinking the
  label to fit; `copies:`, `duplex:` and `pick_tray_by_size:` are hints too
  (see [../reference/printing.md](../reference/printing.md)).
- In a spec: `have_pdf_colors("#000000", "#FFFFFF")` holds the label to black and white (the
  white is the service's text on its black box), and `have_page_count(1)` to one label.
